<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#vergobret">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vergobret</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as uergobretos, uercobretos, vergobretus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The vergobret was the chief magistrate among certain Gallic peoples, described by Caesar among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> in 58 and 52 BCE and attested after the Roman conquest on coins and in inscriptions; it is the only Gaulish magistracy recorded at once in texts, on coins and in inscriptions. Among the Aedui the vergobret was elected for one year with regal authority, and Caesar says he held the power of life and death, though major decisions likely rested with a council of nobles. Two living members of one family could not both hold the office, and the holder could not leave the tribe's territory. In 52 BCE Caesar settled a disputed election in favour of Convictolitavis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vergobret.jpg" alt="Vergobret" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lexoviens (peuple celtique de Gaule). Autorité émettrice de monnaie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Bronze,_fonds_celtique,_Lexoviens_-_btv1b11289265h_(1_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Julius Caesar describes states, civitates, with three classes, knights, Druids and commoners, and by about 50 BCE some tribes had replaced kingship with elected magistrates, the <b>Vergobrets</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Society</a></li></ul></section>
